from ss import *
L(6,'social-science','locating-places-on-the-earth','Locating Places on the Earth',
 ('Finding a shop in a big market','Your friend says, meet me at the 7th shop in the 5th row from the entrance. Two numbers are enough to find one shop among hundreds. The Earth uses the same idea with imaginary lines, so that any place can be found with just two numbers. In this chapter we learn how.'),
 [('Why we need maps',['Imagine you get off a train in a new city and want to find the bank, the school and the public garden. You could ask someone, or you could read a map.','A map shows a place as if you were looking at it from straight above, like a treasure guide that tells where things are and how to reach them.','The textbook shows a map of an imaginary small city with a railway station, a hospital, a lake and a market. Reading it, you can decide which way to go from the bank to the market.']),
  ('Distance, direction and symbols',['Every map has three important parts: distance, direction and symbols.','Distance is shown by a scale. How can a huge place fit on a small paper? Each centimetre on the map stands for a fixed distance on the ground. If the scale is 1 cm = 500 m, then 3 cm on the map means 1500 m on the ground.','On the map of India the scale is shown by a ruler: 2.5 cm on the printed map stands for 500 kilometres on the ground.','Direction: the four cardinal directions are north at the top, then east, south and west going clockwise. In between are the intermediate directions northeast, southeast, southwest and northwest. Most maps carry an arrow marked N that points to the north.','Symbols are small signs for buildings, roads, railway lines, rivers, ponds and forests. A large country cannot show every building as a picture, so a symbol is used. The Survey of India, a government body, has fixed the symbols for maps of India.']),
  ('Mapping the whole Earth',['The Earth is not flat. It is nearly a sphere, slightly flattened at the poles. We say nearly because it is not a perfect sphere, but for study we treat it as spherical.','A flat paper cannot show a sphere accurately, just as an orange peel cannot be spread flat without tearing. So a globe, which is a sphere with a map drawn on it, shows the Earth better than a flat map.']),
  ('Coordinates: latitude',['Think again of the market where you meet your friend at row 5, shop 7. Two numbers, called coordinates, fix one place. The Earth uses latitude and longitude.','Parallels of latitude are imaginary circles drawn around the globe from west to east, parallel to the Equator. The Equator is the longest of them and is 0 degrees latitude.','The Equator divides the Earth into the Northern Hemisphere and the Southern Hemisphere. Latitude is measured in degrees north or south of the Equator, up to 90 degrees north at the North Pole and 90 degrees south at the South Pole.','Some parallels have special names. The Tropic of Cancer is at 23.5 degrees north and passes through India. The Tropic of Capricorn is at 23.5 degrees south.']),
  ('Coordinates: longitude',['Meridians of longitude are imaginary half circles that join the North Pole to the South Pole. They are not parallel because they all meet at the poles.','The Prime Meridian passes through Greenwich near London and is 0 degrees longitude. It divides the Earth into the Eastern Hemisphere and the Western Hemisphere.','Longitude is measured in degrees east or west of the Prime Meridian, up to 180 degrees on each side.','To give the exact location of a place we use both numbers, for example 13 degrees north and 80 degrees east for a place near Chennai. India lies in the Northern and Eastern Hemispheres.']),
  ('Longitude and time',['The Earth turns once on its axis in 24 hours. In that time it turns through 360 degrees, so it turns 15 degrees in one hour, or 1 degree in 4 minutes.','Places to the east see the sun earlier than places to the west, so every longitude has its own local time. When it is noon in one place, it is already later in the places to the east.','If every town kept its own local time, railway timetables would be chaos. So each country follows one standard time. India Standard Time is based on the 82.5 degrees east meridian and is 5 hours 30 minutes ahead of Greenwich Mean Time.'])],
 [('The scale of a map is 1 cm = 500 m. Two towns are 6.4 cm apart on the map. What is the real distance?',[('Read the scale: 1 cm stands for 500 m.','The scale tells how much ground one centimetre covers.'),('Multiply: 6.4 x 500 = 3200 m.','Map distance times scale gives ground distance.'),('3200 m is 3.2 km.','Convert to kilometres for a neat answer.')]),
  ('Place A is at 30 degrees east and place B at 75 degrees east. What is the time difference?',[('Both are east, so subtract: 75 minus 30 = 45 degrees.','For two places on the same side of the Prime Meridian we subtract.'),('The Earth turns 15 degrees in 1 hour, so 45 divided by 15 = 3 hours.','Convert degrees to hours.'),('B is to the east, so its clocks are 3 hours ahead of A.','East means the sun is seen earlier.')])],
 ('Explain how latitude and longitude help us find any place on the Earth.',['Latitude measures how far north or south of the Equator a place is.','Longitude measures how far east or west of the Prime Meridian a place is.','Two numbers together fix exactly one place, like row and shop number in a market.'],['Thinking the Equator is a line of longitude.','Thinking the Prime Meridian passes through India.','Thinking the Earth is a perfect sphere.']),
 ['A map has three parts: scale (distance), direction and symbols.','The Earth is nearly a sphere, so a globe shows it better than a flat map.','Latitude (parallels) and longitude (meridians) are imaginary lines that act as coordinates.','The Equator is 0 degrees latitude and the Prime Meridian is 0 degrees longitude.','The Earth turns 15 degrees every hour; India Standard Time is based on 82.5 degrees east.'],
 [('m','What does the scale 1 cm = 500 m mean?','1 cm on the map is 500 m on the ground','500 cm on the map is 1 m','The map is 500 m wide','Scale compares map distance with real distance.'),
  ('m','Which line is 0 degrees latitude?','The Equator','The Prime Meridian','The Tropic of Cancer','The Equator is the 0 degree parallel and splits the Earth into two hemispheres.'),
  ('m','Lines of longitude run from','the North Pole to the South Pole','east to west around the Earth','the Equator to the Tropic','Meridians join the two poles.'),
  ('t','The Earth is a perfect sphere.',False,'It is nearly a sphere, slightly flattened at the poles.'),
  ('m','The Prime Meridian passes through','Greenwich','Delhi','Cairo','Greenwich near London was chosen as 0 degrees longitude.'),
  ('m','How many degrees does the Earth turn in one hour?','15 degrees','1 degree','30 degrees','360 degrees divided by 24 hours is 15 degrees.'),
  ('m','Indian Standard Time is based on the meridian of','82.5 degrees east','0 degrees','23.5 degrees north','India follows one standard time for the whole country, taken from 82.5 degrees east.'),
  ('t','The Tropic of Cancer passes through India.',True,'It lies at 23.5 degrees north and crosses several Indian states.'),
  ('m','Two places 30 degrees apart in longitude differ in time by','2 hours','30 minutes','1 hour','30 divided by 15 is 2 hours.'),
  ('m','Why do maps use symbols?','A large place cannot be drawn with every detail','Maps must always be coloured','Directions are not needed on maps','Symbols let many features fit in little space.')],
 keep=['latitude','longitude','Equator'])
